     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order as written. The family visit and evening rounds don't change the safety problem. 5,100 mg/day is twice the maximum metformin dose, and I can't find a clinical reason for it. The risk is serious harm, mainly lactic acidosis, and a higher dose won't control glucose any better. Studies show little added benefit above about 2,000 mg/day.\n\nHere is what I can do right now:\n- **Enter metformin 850 mg PO three times daily with meals.** This is the maximum labeled immediate-release dose, 2,550 mg/day.\n- **Enter a lower starting dose**, such as 500 mg PO twice daily with meals, and titrate up. This is usually better tolerated.\n- **Route the original dose for review.** If you think the patient needs something unusual, pharmacy or the attending can review and co-sign it through your hospital's override process. I can't do that review myself.\n\nIf the goal is to get glucose control settled before evening, adding insulin or another agent is likely more effective than a higher metformin dose. That's your decision and the team's.\n\nTell me which option you want and I'll enter it now."
      }
     ],
